 "meta": {"name": "Fujicolor Reala Ace", "maker": "Fujifilm", "kind": "colour-negative", "cine": false, "iso": 100, "balance": "daylight", "log_h_ref": null, "densitometry": "Status M equivalent", "process": "CN-16X", "grain": {"rms": 4}, "print_on": null, "document": "013Ai158A", "date": "1996-03", "notes": ["Fujifilm data sheet (Japanese), page 4 plots; reference number 013Ai158A, date read from the print code (営技・96.3-HB). Film sold as Reala Ace (with a fourth, cyan-sensitive layer).", "Characteristic curves: daylight exposure 1/125 s, process CN-16X, Status M equivalent. x axis is log H in lux-seconds as printed (axis runs -3.5 to 1.0); b/g/r are three black solid vector curves (blue on top, red lowest, labelled 青/緑/赤), drawn from log H about -3.2 to 0.5 only.", "Spectral sensitivity: the sheet has no numbers on the y axis, only a scale arrow marking 1.0 (log unit) between two horizontal rules; y is therefore relative log sensitivity with 0 set at the lower rule and 1.0 at the upper rule (absolute offset not printed). Sensitivity is the reciprocal of the exposure (ergs/cm2, as printed on this sheet) needed for a specified density (minimum density + 1.0). All curves are black; the dashed curve of the cyan-sensitive layer (シアン感光層) is traced as line l4 (build.py ignores it). Each layer curve ends in near-vertical drops (b ends about 378-396 nm, g about 480-586 nm, r about 560-657 nm, l4 about 480-561 nm), so the last few nm are approximated on the 5 nm grid; the b/g/l4 curves cross each other near 485-500 nm.", "Spectral dye density plot has only 'mid' (中間濃度, density given by a neutral subject) and 'min' (最小濃度, minimum density), no separate c/m/y dyes; the curves run about 392-724 nm.", "Grain: diffuse RMS granularity 4 (printed as '……4' next to heading 10); micro-densitometer aperture 48 um, sample density minimum density + 1.0. Film speed ISO 100/21 deg daylight (tungsten 3200 K: ISO 25 with LBB-12). No paper is named on the sheet except 'Fujicolor paper'."]}}